WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.170
I like to feel that there can be great beauty in medical art.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:15.160
A beauty that really goes hand-and-hand with science, as we explore the infinite inner spaces of the human body.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:33.780
Here we move deeper through the spectacular caverns of the heart and we feel like explorers in this fantastic anatomical world.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:40.300
As the artist, I like to become completely involved in the visual presentation,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:47.920
and my objective is to help the observer become just as involved: personally, artistically, scientifically.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.320
Here are some animated examples from a film.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.110
Now we have the chance to observe heart function in a large variety of ways.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:36.160
The value of animation as an art form is that it's a controlled medium.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.100
We can show a specific action in time and space.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:57.140
And we can have the advantage of being able to expand or condense this time in order to get the information across.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:09.860
There's a great value, and a lot of satisfaction, in being able to adjust our concept,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:16.280
frame-by-frame, as one would with brushstrokes on a painting.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:21.840
Ever since man lived in caves, there were artists concerned with depicting scientific truth.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.470
Now, these Lascaux cave painters felt that the success of the hunt

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.860
depended on their ability to portray their animal prey as realistically as possible.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.780
There are some Australian aborigines that use an x-ray approach.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:49.140
They get involved with the total animal, including the internal organs.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:03:01.160
Back in medieval times, these were almost as primitive in their concept, but they did represent the technology of the age.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.240
Then came the great Leonardo da Vinci: painter, sculptor, architect, engineer, and scientist.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.440
His anatomical art was probably his greatest contribution to the scientific field.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:30.040
This microscopic anatomy was done by the Dutchman Jan Swammerdam, 100 years before the invention of the microscope.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:36.540
Now, the works of Albert Durer were not scientific in the literal sense,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:42.980
but they reflect tremendous curiosity for exact detail--even to the sore spots of his own body.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.920
Here's the famous Primavera by Botticelli.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.480
What infinite pains the artist took to paint these flowers in perfect botanical accuracy.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.320
During the 18th to 19th centuries, the artist-scientists portrayed more accurately than ever

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.040
the anatomical discoveries of their day.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.040
Now we're back in our time.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:16.960
The eye, for example, can take on a new look, and today I think we can stimulate the viewer

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.640
and create more visual excitement than ever.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:34.800
We can explore the middle ear in terms of its own ethereal space, its intricate forms, its practical sculptural beauty.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.460
Sometimes information has to be explained in an exaggerated manner

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.960
because it's too compact to visualize in its normal state.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:08.200
Or, sometimes it has to be portrayed in non-realistic colors, so that we can reveal its inner structure more clearly.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:24.720
At other times, we have the opportunity to take an artistic journey on film,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.100
using the vehicle of artwork and camera mechanics.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:35.460
Here, as we move through the lung structure, we can see its beauty in a normal state.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:52.140
Or we can feel the chaos of it, in an abnormal state, by man's abuse of his own lungs to the point of destruction.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:06.180
What might look like apparent chaos in the area of head and neck anatomy became for me an organized graphic adventure.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:18.590
As for the variety of realism in the oral cavity and its related structures, this makes its own demands on the artist.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:48.040
What I found unique about Walt Disney was his genius to convey nature's drama in its many forms.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:06.280
In this case, we were designing for schoolchildren, and the purpose was to create an inner world of wonder

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:11.000
away from the anatomical look as they watched the development of the human fetus.

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:11.500
In a film called Fantastic Voyage, tens of millions of people were exposed for the first time

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.640
to the unbelievable wonders of their own body.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:30.060
Within the demands of a storyline, the big challenge for us was to make the anatomy as authentic as possible.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:55.640
Here's another fantastic voyage: slicing through the human brain using stop-motion photography.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:18.960
In this sales piece, my goal was to make the presentation as beautiful as it is informative.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.740
Today, in the field of medical communication,

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:49.240
I feel very strongly that the concept of informative beauty is a goal worth striving for.

